<div class="article-body">

<p>Your lettuce is bolting because two things happened at the same time: the water or air around it got warm, and the light day got long. That is the whole trigger. Bolting is not a disease, it is not a nutrient problem, and it is not damage you can undo. It is the plant finishing its life and switching from making leaves to making flowers. Once the center stem stretches up, no fix brings the head back.</p>

<p>Here is why I care about this more than any other lettuce problem. Bitterness arrives before the stem does. I have cut leaves off a plant that looked perfectly normal and found them tasting like a dandelion stem, and three days later the same plant pushed up a flower shoot. The stalk is not the warning. It is the receipt.</p>

<p>I ran four trays on one closed shelf last summer and kept a cheap thermometer in each reservoir, writing the number on masking tape every morning. The tray sitting next to the light driver hit 79F and bolted at day 31. The tray at the other end of the same shelf, same seed, same feed, sat at 68 to 71F and went 58 days before it committed. Four feet of shelf, eleven days of difference, and the only thing that changed was the water temperature.</p>

<h2 id="what">1. What Bolting Actually Is (It Is Not a Stress Symptom)</h2>

<p>Lettuce is a cool-season annual. It grows leaves while conditions say "keep going," then it commits to reproduction and quits the leaf business for good. Those are two separate events, and the science keeps them separate too: floral transition and stem elongation are distinguishable processes that overlap in their genetics. A 2026 review in Plant Biotechnology Reports puts the drivers as heat-responsive transcription factors, the flowering gene LsSOC1, and both auxin and gibberellin signaling. Translation: the plant is not limping. It is executing a plan.</p>

<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> the tall stem is a hormone event, not a resource problem. That matters because it kills the most common theory beginners have. People look at a bolting plant and think "it is stressed and starving, so it is rushing to make seed." Feed it more, and it still bolts, because you cannot out-fertilize a hormone signal. You can only stop sending the signal.</p>

<p>The other half of the trigger is day length. Lettuce is a long-day plant, so hours matter more than intensity. If you are still running your lights for 18 or 20 hours because "more light equals more growth," you have accidentally told a cool-season plant that it is July. The <a href="/posts/how-much-light-does-lettuce-need">light hours lettuce actually wants indoors</a> is a much shorter day than most first-timers run.</p>

<p>One more thing worth knowing: bolting is not always the heat you can feel right now. A cold snap during the seedling stage can also flip immature plants into flowering later on. That is why two trays that shared the same room for 6 weeks can bolt 3 weeks apart. The trigger was logged when the plants were two inches tall.</p>

<h2 id="temps">2. The 2 Temperatures That Trigger It Indoors</h2>

<p>Outdoors, bolting is an air temperature story. Indoors, in a hydroponic system, it is usually a <em>water</em> temperature story, and that is the part almost nobody checks. Your reservoir is a heat sink. It sits under the light, next to the pump, and against a wall, and it slowly climbs all afternoon while the room itself still reads comfortable on the wall thermostat.</p>

